*Executive summary.* When a tube preamplifier, studio console or vintage hi-fi unit fails, a technician must find which of its many parts failed; specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for that evaluation << cite('rates_studio_doctors', 'rates_archer_hifi', 'rates_cw_guitar_audio') >>. For a circuit it has a model of (@fig:concept), Differential simulates how every cataloged part failure changes the readings, keeps a probability for every group of suspects that no reading can tell apart, and recommends the measurement expected to narrow them most per unit of effort. A language model reads the complaint and explains; readings come from the technician; safety locks run in code. In a pilot on simulated units it named the group containing the failed part first on << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> of as-new and << pct(bt.T6.estimate, 1) >> of aged units, with << num(bt.T9.estimate, 2) >> times half-split tracing's effort to a confirmed answer. Success, fixed before any test unit is run (@tab:targets), needs at least << T['T1'].pass_count >> of << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> simulated units right, << T['T6'].pass_count >> of << num(ev.aged_cases, 0) >> aged ones and << T['T7'].pass_count >> of << ev.fault_board_min >> faults on real boards, plus margins over scripts and a chat model. Boards, interviews and a technician study will test what simulation cannot.

