  image("/results/figures/concept.svg", width: 56%),
  caption: [Differential in one picture. Offline, each circuit and its fault catalog are simulated with SPICE (circuit-simulation software) under part tolerances, as-new and aged, and learned as fault signatures. Online, a Bayesian loop picks each measurement; the language model (LLM) reads the complaint, proposes meter-photo readings for the technician to confirm and explains. Safety locks and the check that every number traces to a measurement run in code.],
) <fig:concept>

<< part(1, "Problem, stakeholders and industry context") >>

*The problem.* Studio consoles, tube preamplifiers and vintage hi-fi are repaired long after their makers stop supporting them, and finding the failed part is the slow step: U.S. defense maintainability guidance holds that most repair time typically goes to fault isolation << cite('waru_acquipedia_rtat') >>. Specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for a bench evaluation << cite('rates_studio_doctors', 'rates_archer_hifi', 'rates_cw_guitar_audio') >>, and one bills an hour even when no fault is found << cite('rates_british_audio') >>; these are examples, not a survey.

*Size of the need.* No statistic counts audio repair: its U.S. industry code was merged into a broader one in 2022 << cite('census_naics_2017_to_2022_concordance') >>, and the closest occupation (24,720 people in May 2023) mostly installs, in retail and construction << cite('bls_oews_492097_2023', 'onet_492097_details') >>. The broader electrical and electronics repair group (117,700 jobs in 2025) is projected to grow 2% to 2035, below the 3.5% average, most openings replacing leavers << cite('bls_ooh_eeir', 'bls_ep_2025_35') >>. Only press reports speak for the specialist niche: skilled hi-fi technicians "are scarce, and their numbers are dwindling" << cite('stereophile_retales_13') >>, and a Chicago shop for old audio gear shrank from seven technicians to two << cite('blockclub_electronic_engineers_2026') >>. An AI assistant raised support agents' productivity 15%, most for the less experienced << cite('brynjolfsson_genai_at_work_2023') >>: at the bench, a case for supporting newer technicians' judgment, not replacing it.

*Where it pays.* A model needs the schematic transcribed (we will time one) and, for our << cs.components >>-part channel strip, << m('library.setup_cost.channel_strip.total_minutes') | round | int >> minutes of computer time, so it pays where one model serves many units, such as a console of identical channel strips.

*Where current AI stands.* Maintenance AI retrieves documents: service histories << cite('ptc_servicemax_ai_2025') >>, manuals << cite('msft_d365_field_service_page') >>, maintenance guidance << cite('siemens_maintenance_copilot_2025') >>, agentic workflows << cite('ibm_mas_92_2026') >> and repair guides << cite('ifixit_fixbot_2025') >>; none of these five simulates the circuit. The best chat model scores 95.50% on textbook analog questions, graded by another language model << cite('razavi_bench_2026') >>, but at the bench parts sit anywhere within tolerance, faults look alike, measurements cost effort and some points carry lethal voltage. A simulation loop re-checking answers lifted a model from 79.52% to 97.59% on undergraduate circuit problems << cite('e2e_circuit_analysis_2025') >>; Differential brings that check to diagnosis.

*Jurisdiction and rules.* We design for the U.S. (the team is in New Jersey) and check EU rules for later sale. No U.S. AI statute covers such a tool; the Administration has asked Congress to preempt state AI laws << cite('wh_ai_legislative_framework_2026') >>, and Colorado's law covers automated decisions about people, such as employment, from 2027 << cite('co_sb26_189') >>, one reason the license forbids rating staff. The EU AI Act would not class a bench aid as high-risk unless shops rated technicians or trainees with it << cite('eu_ai_act_text_mirror') >>; users must be told they deal with an AI from 2 August 2026 << cite('ec_ip_26_1714') >>, and the 2026 amendments keep a duty on providers and deployers to support their staff's AI literacy << cite('eu_ai_omnibus_2026_1744') >>.

