*Interviews before M2*, after an ethics-board determination: six 45-minute interviews (three technicians from independent audio shops, a trainee, a shop owner, a vintage-gear owner), recruited through shops and audio societies, not employers, on diagnosis time, recurring faults, judging readings, what a tool may do and consent to part removal; and 50 anonymized tickets from one shop, coded for faults and diagnosis time.

#decision("The primary user is a qualified bench technician")[
*Options:* a consumer self-repair assistant or a professional bench tool. *Choice:* professional: diagnosis often means testing a powered, open chassis, and << hv.hv_normal + hv.hv_under_fault >> of the channel strip's << cs.test_points >> test points can exceed 50 V (@tab:library). OSHA requires employers to de-energize live parts unless testing needs power (parts under 50 V excepted if that adds no burn or arc-blast risk) and to let only qualified persons work on or test them << cite('osha_1910_333', 'osha_1910_331_399', 'osha_1910_334') >>; the self-employed fall outside OSHA << cite('ecfr_29_1904_31') >>, so the license asks every user for that qualification. *Consequence:* licensed to repair businesses; trainees supervised; mains-side work refused. Hobbyists keep forums and general chat assistants, which follow content policies but hold no hazard map of the unit on the bench.
]

<< part(2, "How diagnosis is done today, and the data landscape") >>

*Today's practice* (the interviews will check it): confirm the complaint; look for burnt or bulging parts; check the supplies; follow a test tone stage by stage, often halving the search ("half-split tracing"); compare voltages with the manual's chart or the healthy channel; test tubes and electrolytics; unsolder a suspect and confirm the fault clears. The hard judgment is which reading is really "off": parts sit anywhere within tolerance, and aging electrolytics lose capacitance and gain series resistance << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>. Replacing every electrolytic ("recapping") often cures a symptom but costs parts and time, risks the board and never names the failed part.

*The information is uneven.* Manuals with schematics and voltage charts exist for many classic models, but units are often modified or undocumented. U.S. repair laws cover newer digital products, not vintage analog gear: New York's took effect in December 2023 << cite('ny_gbl_399nn', 'ny_ag_repair_2024') >>, and five other states followed with limits by date or product << cite('ca_prc_42488', 'co_hb24_1121', 'or_sb1596_2024', 'wa_eshb1483_2025', 'tx_hb2963_2025') >>; the EU's obligation to repair covers only its Annex II products, such as washing machines << cite('eu_r2r_directive_2024_1799', 'ec_r2r_qa_2024') >>. So Differential needs a model per product, built from the schematic, and trusts its hazard map only once the model's healthy readings match the manual's chart; until then every point of a high-voltage unit counts as high voltage. "No single fault fits" covers modified units; units without a schematic are out of scope.

*Labeled fault data is scarce, so we simulate it.* Confirming a failed part takes unsoldering and is rarely recorded; shop logs are private and, after recapping, unlabeled. We know of no public dataset of bench readings with confirmed failed parts; published methods use benchmark filters << cite('gao_yang_jiang_2021_incipient_tim') >> and simulated data << cite('cloete_stander_wilke_2022_access') >>. Analog-test research built simulated fault dictionaries << cite('bandler_salama_1985') >>, found faults the test points cannot tell apart (ambiguity groups) << cite('stenbakken_souders_stewart_1989') >> and applied machine learning << cite('he_he_li_2020_gan_tim') >>; we follow it (@tab:library). A simulated unit draws every part within its tolerance, the mains within ±<< m('library.instrument.mains_tol_pct') | round | int >>%, and one cataloged fault (an open, short, drift, or a mode such as a leaky transistor or worn tube). Since units reaching a bench are old, << design.simulation.aging.training_share_pct | round | int >>% of training units are also aged: electrolytics up to << m('library.aging.electrolytic_c_loss_max_pct') | round | int >>% low with up to << m('library.aging.electrolytic_esr_max_x') | round | int >>× the series resistance, resistors up to << m('library.aging.resistor_drift_max_pct') | round | int >>% high, tubes up to << m('library.aging.triode_emission_loss_max_pct') | round | int >>% weaker. Each reading carries instrument error: a << m('library.instrument.dmm_input_mohm') | round | int >> MΩ meter loads each DC node << cite('fluke_11x_extended_specs') >> and reads within ±(<< num(m('library.instrument.dmm_pct'), 1) >>% + << m('library.instrument.dmm_counts') >> counts) << cite('fluke_117_technical_data') >>, an oscilloscope within ±<< num(m('library.instrument.scope_pct'), 0) >>% of full scale << cite('rigol_ds1000z_user_guide_ch18') >>. The library holds << num(m('library.simulations.total_draws'), 0) >> units (<< m('library.simulations.failed_draws') >> failed to converge). Hand calculations reproduce all << m('hand_calcs.rows_checked') >> healthy bias points and gains checked, each within a tolerance set beforehand. For faults, an audit re-solved << fa.faults >> randomly drawn faults (up to two per fault type and circuit) with a solver we wrote from the published device equations, without the simulator: all << fa.faults_agree >> agreed within twice the meter's accuracy (95% interval << pct(fa.ci95[0], 0) >>-100%), after a first run in which 6 of 42 disagreed through the audit solver's own omissions. That checks the simulator, not reality; the boards test reality. A complaint alone is weak: on the channel strip a hum complaint fits << m('library.symptoms.channel_strip.faults_per_symptom.hum') >> faults.

