*How it will be measured.* The test set holds << num(ev.single_fault_cases, 0) >> single-fault units (<< ev.channel_strip_cases >> channel strip, << ev.cases_per_block >> per block) and << num(ev.aged_cases, 0) >> aged units with the same mix, on seeds disjoint from training. Each case draws a fault uniformly and keeps the unit only if it shows a symptom, else draws again, so faults count by how often they cause a symptom (T14 reweights to a capacitor-heavy mix). Complaints come from a phrase bank the reader was not developed on (its score was seen once, before the rules last changed; T18 uses a sealed bank), in three voices, never naming a part, each symptom dropped with probability << m('library.complaint_noise.p_omit_pct') | round | int >>% and others added with << m('library.complaint_noise.p_add_pct') | round | int >>%. Stress sets: << ev.unmodeled_cases >> units outside the catalog (double faults, wrong-value parts, solder bridges) and tolerances 1.5-3 times wider (a curve). Baselines: a fixed-order service chart (supplies, output, signal, DC, hum, response), half-split tracing (supplies, then halving the signal path with a tone, then DC at the first bad stage; the strongest script) and random probing (never unsolders) share the engine's inference and stopping rule, and the scripts unsolder their leading suspect at << pct(m('library.design.confirm_at'), 0) >> belief; a language model, alone and with our simulator as a tool, picks from the same list on << ev.llm_subset >> stratified units and stops when it names an answer. For T7 a teammate who does not record inserts << ev.fault_board_min >> faults in sealed random order into three 24 V line-driver boards. Study S1 will time at least six technicians with and without the tool, without a bar: six people cannot show a benefit reliably.

*How the bars were set; no test unit has been run.* A pilot simulated for this alone (<< m('pilot2.n_cases.channel_strip') >> channel-strip and << m('pilot2.n_cases.driver') >> block units each, as-new and aged; << m('bars.targets.T13.n_pilot') | int >> out-of-catalog), weighted like the test set, gave: right group first on << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> of as-new and << pct(bt.T6.estimate, 1) >> of aged units, a << num(((100 * bt.T1.estimate) | round(1)) - ((100 * bt.T6.estimate) | round(1)), 1) >>-point cost of aging (<< pct(bt.T6.aged_flag_rate, 1) >> of aged units flagged "no single fault fits", << pct(bt.T6.aged_wrong_name_rate, 1) >> misnamed). <% set ep = eff.engine.probing | round(1) %><% set eu = (eff.engine.unsoldering + eff.engine.confirming_charge) | round(1) %><% set hp = eff.half_split.probing | round(1) %><% set hu = (eff.half_split.unsoldering + eff.half_split.confirming_charge) | round(1) %>To a confirmed answer, the engine alone spent << num(ep + eu, 1) >> units (<< num(ep, 1) >> probing, << num(eu, 1) >> unsoldering) and half-split tracing << num(hp + hu, 1) >> (<< num(hp, 1) >> and << num(hu, 1) >>). Reading the complaint moved accuracy from << pct(m('bars.context.accuracy_weighted.engine'), 1) >> to << pct(bt.T1.estimate, 1) >> and effort by << num(-m('bars.context.complaint_effort_saving_pct'), 1) >>%, so the reader must still prove itself (T11, T18). <% set pc = targets.targets | selectattr('primary') | selectattr('chance_met', 'defined') | map(attribute='chance_met') | list %>Each bar is the pilot estimate moved << m('bars.k') >> standard errors against the system and rounded, never past the estimate; counting the pilot's own error, each primary then has a << (100 * (pc | min)) | round | int >>-<< (100 * (pc | max)) | round | int >>% chance of passing. T5 has no pilot without an API key: +10 points is the smallest margin we judged would justify a model per circuit over a general one. T7's 70% follows from what a pass must show: << T['T7'].pass_