*Scope.* In: single static faults (opens, shorts, drift, wear) and the hum and distortion they cause, in supplies, preamplifier and line-level stages. Out: power amplifiers, intermittent and mechanical faults (static simulation cannot produce them), double faults (flagged only), mains-side work, units without a schematic, unattended probing.

#decision("Score by ambiguity group and effort to a confirmed answer")[
*Options:* per-part accuracy and a count of measurements, or group accuracy and weighted effort to a confirmed answer. *Choice:* the latter: per-part scoring rewards lucky guesses between faults with identical readings, unsoldering is not like touching a probe, and an answer is worth acting on only once confirmed. *Consequence:* group sizes (@tab:library) and raw effort are reported too.
]

<< part(4, "Preliminary AI approach, risks and ethics") >>

*Why a hybrid.* Language models guess when uncertain, partly because training and evaluation reward guessing << cite('kalai_why_hallucinate_2025') >>; the 97.59% system of Part 1 needed a simulation check. Simulation supplies the physics; Bayesian inference keeps a probability for every suspect group, updated after each reading through likelihoods learned from simulated units: one to three full-covariance Gaussians per fault (chosen by BIC) over the circuit's readings, giving a likelihood for any subset of readings in closed form. "No single fault fits" is a hypothesis with a broad flat density, calibrated on out-of-catalog units to a << pct(m('library.design.false_alarm_budget'), 0) >> false-alarm budget. Boosted trees << cite('ke_2017_lightgbm') >> are a comparison engine (pilot top-1 << pct(m('bars.context.engine_disc_top1_weighted'), 1) >> against << pct(m('bars.context.accuracy_weighted.engine'), 1) >>). The next measurement maximizes expected information gain per unit of effort, the one-step entropy rule of model-based diagnosis << cite('dekleer_williams_1987') >> with a cost term (an optimal sequence needs a search << cite('pattipati_alexandridis_1990') >>); a diagnosis stops at << pct(m('library.design.stop_threshold'), 0) >> belief in one group, at the budget, or when nothing informative remains. The language model (Anthropic's Claude, version pinned) reads complaints (offline, rules do), proposes meter-photo readings for confirmation and explains; every number it writes must trace to a reading (T17), and it stays only if T5, T18 and T19 show it helps.

*Set aside:* consistency-based candidates << cite('dekleer_williams_1987') >> (tolerances blur conflicts); retrieval over manuals (they describe healthy units); hand-built troubleshooting networks (one per product, no ground truth); deep networks, which can condition on any subset of readings << cite('ivanov_vaeac_2019', 'li_acflow_2020') >> and choose measurements by information gain << cite('ma_eddi_2019') >>, but need more data and are harder to audit than one density per fault.

*Autonomy.* By the functions automated << cite('parasuraman_sheridan_wickens_2000') >>, we define five levels, named by analogy to driving automation: L0, the technician does everything; L1, the tool ranks suspects; L2, it also recommends each measurement; L3, it takes readings through instruments, a person approving each; L4, an unattended fixture. Differential sits at L2: a person chooses, takes and confirms every measurement, since a wrong step near high voltage can injure.

*Safety.* A fault can put high voltage where there is normally none: channel-strip point << hv.showcase.tp >>, normally about << num(hv.showcase.normal_max_v, 0) >> V, reaches << num(hv.showcase.worst_case_v, 0) >> V under "<< hv.showcase.fault_label >>". Steps at points that can exceed 50 V carry hands-off and discharge instructions; in a high-voltage chassis every powered step says "high voltage inside" and names a meter rated << design.safety.meter_rating >>, and powered steps wait until the technician records a bring-up through a variac or series lamp. Before a part is removed, the owner's approval is recorded and the technician records under << num(m('library.design.discharge_verify_max_v'), 0) >> V (our margin) at every point that can hold high voltage, which catches a capacitor left charged behind a failed-open resistor; the check lapses after << design.safety.discharge_valid_min | round | int >> minutes without a bleeder, as a discharged capacitor recovers charge << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>, and on any power-up. These are checks, not guarantees: readings are the technician's attestation, and the hazard map covers only the modeled circuit; T15 checks every possible step.

*People.* Runner-up suspects and reasons are shown, yet AI can mislead: consultants given GPT-4 were 19 percentage points less likely to solve a task outside its competence << cite('dellacqua_jagged_frontier_2023') >>, and those trusting AI more report less critical thinking << cite('lee_critical_thinking_2025') >>. Trainees commit their own next step before seeing the tool's and need a named supervisor near high voltage; a tutor giving hints instead of answers largely mitigated the learning loss an unrestricted chat model caused << cite('bastani_genai_learning_2025') >>. S1 plants one wrong recommendation on a non-hazardous step of a 24 V board, after an ethics-board determination; participants are told beforehand that some advice may be wrong on purpose, are debriefed, and are recruited outside their employers. Tickets record roles, not names, and the license forbids rating staff; whether owners turn faster diagnosis into a faster pace is a question for the interviews. As fairness audits do for groups of people << cite('buolamwini_gebru_2018') >>, errors will be broken down by complaint voice, fault type and circuit; a gap above 5 points on a primary blocks release until explained.

*Accountability.* The team answers for the hazard map, stated performance and corrections; a shop, for its circuit models and staff; the technician, for measurements and precautions. Whether advice software is a product (strict liability) or a service (negligence) is unsettled in the U.S., where bills would make AI systems products << cite('us_ai_lead_act_s2937', 'us_s2937_ai_lead_act_billstatus') >> or shield developers when licensed professionals use them << cite('us_rise_act_s2081') >>; the revised EU directive treats software placed on the market after 9 December 2026 as a product << cite('eu_pld_2024_2853') >>.

